Very revealing! Looking at the test on line 51-73 (`clifftop_retreat_enters_untapped_with_mountain`):

```rust
// Put a Mountain on the battlefield.
let mountain_id = reg.get_id_by_name("Mountain").unwrap();
let mtn = state.create_object(mountain_id, P0, Zone::Battlefield, None, None);
state.get_object_mut(mtn).unwrap().name = "Mountain".into();
state.get_object_mut(mtn).unwrap().subtypes = vec!["Mountain".into()];


```

The tests are manually patching subtypes onto the objects as a workaround—the real issue is that when lands enter play through normal gameplay via `PlayLand`, the `move_object` call doesn't populate subtypes from the registry, leaving them empty. So the tests are compensating for this gap by hand-setting the subtypes, but actual game objects would have `subtypes = vec![]` and fail the subtype checks.

The core problem is that `controller_has_matching_land` only checks `obj.subtypes`, which never gets populated during normal play, meaning Hinterland Harbor would always enter tapped in real gameplay even when you control a Forest or Island.

```

rust
obj.subtypes` either...

Let me check what actually happens in `setup_game` when creating card objects.

Looking at the setup code, I can see it's copying over colors, name, keywords, and card types, but it's not copying the subtypes from `card_data` to the object. So when a Forest gets added to the library and later played through normal gameplay, its `obj.subtypes` stays empty, which means `controller_has_matching_land` won't find it since it only checks `obj.subtypes` and not the registry data.

This is definitely the root cause—Hinterland Harbor will always enter tapped because it can't detect the basic lands that were set up this way. I should check if Clifftop Retreat has the same problem to see if this is a widespread pattern.

